  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Cash Consortium of Sudan (CCS)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">On behalf of the Cash Consortium of Sudan (CCS), Mercy Corps requests GBP 5,000,000 from FCDO to establish an effective, efficient and people-centred response to the complex humanitarian emergency in Sudan. Through&#x2019;s FCDO contribution, the CCS will reach an estimated 79,014 individuals (i.e. 13,169 households) across 11 localities in 4 states with a harmonised and scaled approach to multi-sectoral cash assistance for basic needs. Targeted locations include both the worst conflict-affected areas, as well as displacement hot spots, with a focus on hotspot areas with IPC4, included in the Sudan Famine Prevention Plan 2024, namely Khartoum, East and South Darfur, and South Kordofan.The Programme&#x2019;s outcome is to enable conflict-affected individuals to meet their basic needs, by providing Multi-Purpose Cash Assistance (MPCA) to vulnerable conflict-affected households to enable them to better meet their critical basic needs and reduce the use of negative coping strategies.The CCS includes 8 international NGOs and 10 national NGOs, selected through an Expression of Interest process on the basis of their geographic and technical complementarity, demonstrated capacities for delivery, and interest in enhancing capacities for cash assistance, and leverages a wide geographic presence to respond to current and emerging needs while avoiding duplication. The scaled and harmonised approach will enable CCS partners to influence the overall response with emphasis on accountability to affected populations, localisation and quality programming, and foundations for nexus over the medium to long term.The CCS is looking at building a portfolio of donors to advance a progressive vision of the potential of cash and markets assistance to transform humanitarian response and recovery in partnership with vulnerable conflict-affected populations in Sudan.The consortium approach will enable CCS partners to act as an engine to build upon existing assistance and generate improved coordination and delivery of harmonised cash and market assistance at scale to respond to current and emerging needs while avoiding duplication. The partnership will draw on learnings and good practices from other global NGO-led platforms for scaled cash delivery to create harmonised approaches that encourage synergies amongst different programs and donors to deliver more impactful, efficient and cost-effective assistance throughout Sudan. The partnership will also focus on documenting learnings to inform the broader response, including thematic areas such as effective program delivery for cash assistance and market support in Sudan, cost-effectiveness of cash modalities in comparison to in-kind, evidence on vulnerability, priority needs, outcomes, and opportunities for innovation to drive impact, such as addressing barriers to digitisation.The scaled and harmonised approach will enable CCS partners to influence the overall response with emphasis on accountability to affected populations, localisation and quality programming, and foundations for nexus over the medium to long term.</narrative>
  </description>
